From 8d284220c6d579e1b2a25d54523db928093bc7e0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Aryan Randeriya <aryan.k.randeriya@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 18:34:25 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 10/18] fix(stealth,js): claim the host OS, add SVG/HTML
 element interfaces, return service worker registrations

Stealth identity: the wreq emulation, UA, navigator.platform and client
hints claimed Windows on every host. From a Linux host that mismatch got
amazon.com's "Continue shopping" captcha (validateCaptcha) on every load,
even after the AWS WAF challenge passed; real Chrome on the same host with
its UA/client hints overridden to Windows got the same captcha on 4 of 5
loads, and 0 of 4 with its own Linux identity. The stealth identity now
follows the target OS (Linux x86_64 / Windows / macOS), and an empty
platformVersion (what Chrome on Linux reports) is no longer replaced.

JS: SvelteKit's router tests `a instanceof SVGAElement` for every link;
the global did not exist, so nytimes.com's embedded Svelte app threw a
ReferenceError at startup. Every SVG element now gets its own interface
by local name (SVG 2 + Filter Effects), and the HTML element interfaces
that were missing (HTMLTableCellElement, HTMLTimeElement, ...) are
distinct subclasses instead of undefined.

navigator.serviceWorker.register() resolved to undefined, so pages that
set registration.onupdatefound threw. It now resolves to a
ServiceWorkerRegistration with no worker (installing/waiting/active null),
enforces same-origin like a browser, and `ready` stays pending as it does
while no worker is active.

Diagnostics: the dynamic-script fetch error names the URL, the stealth
body-read error names the URL, and OBSCURA_RENDER_TIMING reports when
retained styles fall back because of shadow-root stylesheets.

